  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nMostly clear skies will continue across the region into the weekend, \nthanks to persistent high pressure centered nearly overhead. \nDewpoints have been lowered some from initial guidance today and \ntomorrow, in order to reflect current trends. Low RH values \n(generally below 40%) will be observed again each afternoon through \nSaturday, but light winds will limit the risk of fire spread for the \nextent of the remaining dry period. Afternoon high temperatures will \nstay mild through Sunday, with most seeing values 10 to 15 degrees \nabove normal, while morning low temperatures generally begin to tick \nupward into the weekend.\n\nThe timing of the next opportunity for precipitation will depend on \nhow far east or west one is within the CWA. Two approaching systems \nare noted in current modeling: a low pressure system working up the \nsoutheastern coastline over the weekend, and another simultaneously \ncutting northeast into Canada. Some of our mountain counties \nfurthest east may stand a chance to receive some rain early \nSunday from this coastal low, depending on how far inland it \ntracks. Modeling has diverged some on this track, with some \ntaking it further inland and producing a wetter Sunday for those\neastern counties. Low PoPs are maintained at the moment. \n\nMore agreed-upon is the progression of the surface low into Canada, \nwith attendant cold front dragging across the region overnight \nSunday into Monday morning. Meager forcing will likely result in \nonly light rainfall totals through midday Monday. Afternoon \ntemperatures will not recover much from morning lows, thanks to the \npassage of the cold front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nCooler weather settles in for Thanksgiving week, and eyes turn to \nthe potential for a more notable system to produce a round of \nprecipitation over the area as we get into the mid-to-latter portion \nof the week. There is still plenty of disagreement within modeling \non how the holiday travel may be impacted, if at all, but it is \nworth keeping an eye on as we draw closer.",
